%description:

TcpCubic.hystartDetect:none -- the delay detector does not act, the ACK-train detector does not act.

The claim (TcpCubic.ned): "HyStart detectors, as a bitmask: 1 = ACK train, 2 = delay
increase, 3 = both".

The classes: none (0), train (1), delay (2), both (3, the default, the value of Linux
tcp_cubic.c), invalid (below 0, above 3). The code: HyStart acts in slow start when the window is at least hystartLowWindow segments
(TcpCubic::processRttSample()). TcpCubic::hystartUpdate() looks for the exit: detector 1, the
ACK train, and detector 2, the delay increase, whose threshold is the minimum RTT / 8, clamped
to hystartDelayMin and hystartDelayMax (TcpCubic::hystartDelayThresh()). At the exit, ssthresh
becomes the window. TcpCubic::initializeAlgorithm() checks the values. This test separates "none" from the other values: each value gives another pair of results.

Host under test: host1, with TcpCubic and HyStart, which opens two connections. Connection A
at 0.1 s: to host2, where only the delay detector can act; the path is host1 -- tap -- router -- host2, with a bottleneck of 10 Mbps from the router to
host2 and a round trip of 40 ms. host2 has a window of 1000000 bytes and acknowledges each
second segment (the delayed ACK), so its ACKs come 2.4 ms apart, more than the ACK delta of 2
ms: they form no ACK train, and only the queue at the bottleneck can end slow start. Connection B at 2 s: to a TCP
sink on the router, where only the ACK-train detector can act. The path to the router has
100 Mbps and no bottleneck; the router has no delayed ACK, and its window of 260000 bytes is
a little larger than the path holds (about 240000 bytes), so the ACKs come back to back and
the queue stays below the delay threshold of 4 ms. Observation: level 3, at 1.9 s, HyStart of connection A found no exit; at 3.9 s, HyStart of
connection B found no exit. At each time, the newest connection is the one under test.

%# parameter: TcpCubic.hystartDetect
%# class: none
%# value: 0
%# key: *.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.hystartDetect
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpCubicHystartDetectNone")
        .act(action().at(1.9)
                 .describe("connection A, to host2 through the bottleneck")
                 .assertModule("host1.tcp.conn-*", "state.hystart_found == false"))
        .act(action().at(3.9)
                 .describe("connection B, to the router without a queue")
                 .assertModule("host1.tcp.conn-*", "state.hystart_found == false"));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpCubicHystartDetectNone compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.hystartDetect = 0    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass = "TcpCubic"
*.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.hystartEnabled = true
network = inet.protocoltest.TapRouterNetwork
*.linkDelay = 5ms
*.bottleneckDatarate = 10Mbps
*.bottleneckDelay = 10ms
*.host2.tcp.advertisedWindow = 1000000
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 1000000B
*.router.hasTcp = true
*.router.numApps = 1
*.router.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.router.app[0].localPort = 2000
*.router.app[0].echoFactor = 0
*.router.tcp.delayedAcksEnabled = false
*.router.tcp.advertisedWindow = 260000
*.host1.numApps = 2
*.host1.app[1].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[1].connectAddress = "router"
*.host1.app[1].connectPort = 2000
*.host1.app[1].tOpen = 2.0s
*.host1.app[1].tSend = 2.1s
*.host1.app[1].sendBytes = 10000000B
*.host1.app[1].tClose = 1000s
sim-time-limit = 4s
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpCubicHystartDetectNone: PASS
